    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_831cc0661a937c54d3ef05f560c9f5516fe15f9ee581e52b36c2bb098f7dd25c",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Together the units specify a missing representation-and-sign mechanism for an index-theorem route: Weil's criterion requires negativity of a quadratic expression for every admissible convolution-type test function, finite-field geometry realizes the analogous sign as negative semidefiniteness of divisor self-intersection, and the later global discussion asks for classical cohomology, Frobenius, and index-theorem analogues that could supply such a mechanism. The Hilbert–Pólya self-adjoint operator is a parallel conjectural spectral route, not a consequence of the quadratic-form argument.",
